+**`setAlwaysOnTop()` is "Keep on top"**, the same switch as the pin control (`core/always-on-top`) in every window's title bar. A window kept on top paints above every window that is not, even while another window holds focus or a new one opens. Several can be on at once; among themselves they stack in focus order, and turning the flag off drops the window back into normal stacking. Focus is untouched — the focused window is still `getFocused()` and still wears `os-window--focused` when a kept-on-top window paints over it — so no focus or blur events fire. A child of a kept-on-top window rides in the same layer, so it never sinks under the owner it blocks, and a fullscreen window still covers everything while it lasts. The flag is read back as `win.alwaysOnTop`, stamped as the `os-window--always-on-top` class, and saved with the session (`alwaysOnTop: true` on the window's entry), so it survives a reload. Each change fires `os-window-changed` with `reason: 'always-on-top'`.
